2025-12-01 09:32:43,424 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Below is a concise, evidence-backed comparison of two APIs you mentioned: OpenAI (general platform API) and GitHub (repository management). I highlight authentication/security models, concrete repository endpoints with parameters, documented limits/constraints, and documentation inconsistencies you should be aware of. Every claim is tied to a verifiable source from the provided OpenAPI extracts and official docs.

1) Authentication models and security posture

OpenAI API
- Security scheme: ApiKeyAuth (OpenAPI info shows Security: ApiKeyAuth; server https://api.openai.com/v1). Source: OpenAI OpenAPI 3.1.0, v2.3.0 overview (/v1, ApiKeyAuth) — https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference
- Webhooks exist for several event types (e.g., WebhookResponseCompleted, WebhookBatchCompleted, WebhookFineTuningJobSucceeded, WebhookRealtimeCallIncoming). Source: /v1/responses, /v1/realtime/sessions extracts (webhooks navigation group) — https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference

GitHub REST API v3
- OAuth and PAT classic scopes are enforced on repo actions (e.g., create repo requires repo or public_repo scope as applicable). Source: POST /user/repos, POST /orgs/{org}/repos — https://docs.github.com/rest/repos/repos#create-a-repository-for-the-authenticated-user and https://docs.github.com/rest/repos/repos#create-an-organization-repository
- GitHub App flow with short‑lived installation access tokens:
  - Create token: POST /app/installations/{installation_id}/access_tokens; tokens expire in 1 hour; can be limited to specific repositories (by name or ID) up to 500; can down-scope permissions to a subset of the app’s permissions. Authentication to call this endpoint must be as the App (JWT). Source — https://docs.github.com/rest/apps/apps#create-an-installation-access-token-for-an-app
- OAuth App token maintenance endpoints:
  - Check a token: POST /applications/{client_id}/token (invalid tokens return 404; “Authentication behaves differently” is noted). Source — https://docs.github.com/rest/apps/oauth-applications#check-a-token
  - Reset a token: PATCH /applications/{client_id}/token (returns new token). Source — https://docs.github.com/rest/apps/oauth-applications#reset-a-token
  - Delete a token: DELETE /applications/{client_id}/token (204 on success). Source — https://docs.github.com/rest/apps/oauth-applications#delete-an-app-token
- Rate limiting endpoint: GET /rate_limit does not count against your REST rate limit; categories include core, search, code_search, graphql, and more; response headers include X-RateLimit-Limit with example 5000; legacy “rate” object being deprecated in favor of resources.core. Source — https://docs.github.com/rest/rate-limit/rate-limit#get-rate-limit-status-for-the-authenticated-user
- Webhook security: deliveries include X-Hub-Signature-256 (HMAC SHA-256) header for signature verification. Example header documented across webhook sections. Source (x‑webhooks excerpts) — https://docs.github.com/webhooks/webhook-events-and-payloads#branch_protection_configuration and endpoints such as GET /app/hook/config

Security takeaways supported by numbers and constraints
- GitHub App installation token expiration: 1 hour; repository scoping limit: max 500 repos; permission subset scoping supported. Source — apps/create-installation-access-token
- GitHub collaborator invite rate: Max 50 invitations per repository per 24 hours for outside collaborators; no limit for org members. Source — PUT /repos/{owner}/{repo}/collaborators/{username}
- Branch protection update constraints: combined users + apps + teams list capped at 100; required_approving_review_count allowed range 0–6; wildcards not allowed in REST branch path (use GraphQL for wildcard patterns). Sources — PUT /repos/{owner}/{repo}/branches/{branch}/protection; GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/branches/{branch}/protection
- GitHub rate_limit endpoint: does not consume limit; response headers expose limit/remaining/reset (example header limit 5000). Source — GET /rate_limit

2) Repository management endpoints (GitHub) with key parameters/constraints

Listing and fetching repos
- List user repos: GET /user/repos
  - Query: visibility (all|public|private; default all); affiliation (default owner,collaborator,organization_member); type (all|owner|public|private|member; 422 if used with visibility or affiliation); sort (created|updated|pushed|full_name); direction (asc|desc); per_page (default 30, max 100); page; since/before (ISO 8601). Source — https://docs.github.com/rest/repos/repos#list-repositories-for-the-authenticated-user
- List org repos: GET /orgs/{org}/repos
  - Query: type (all|public|private|forks|sources|member; default all); sort (created|updated|pushed|full_name); direction (asc if sort=full_name, else desc); per_page default 30 (max 100); page. Source — https://docs.github.com/rest/repos/repos#list-organization-repositories
- Get a repo: GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}
  - Note: security_and_analysis block requires admin or org owner/security manager. Source — https://docs.github.com/rest/repos/repos#get-a-repository

Create/update/delete/transfer repos
- Create for user: POST /user/repos
  - OAuth/PAT scopes: repo or public_repo as applicable; body includes name (required), private (default false), allow_* merge settings, has_* feature flags, auto_init, license_template, gitignore_template, etc. Source — https://docs.github.com/rest/repos/repos#create-a-repository-for-the-authenticated-user
- Create in org: POST /orgs/{org}/repos
  - Body: name (required), visibility (public|private), delete_branch_on_merge (requires org owner), and same feature/merge settings; org membership required. Source — https://docs.github.com/rest/repos/repos#create-an-organization-repository
- Update repo: PATCH /repos/{owner}/{repo}
  - Supports toggling has_issues/projects/wiki, default_branch, merge strategies, delete_branch_on_merge, visibility (may 422 if org restricts), security_and_analysis enablement (e.g., advanced_security.status="enabled"). Source — https://docs.github.com/rest/repos/repos#update-a-repository
- Delete repo: DELETE /repos/{owner}/{repo}
  - Requires admin; may 403 if org policy forbids; classic PAT/OAuth needs delete_repo scope; 204 on success. Source — https://docs.github.com/rest/repos/repos#delete-a-repository
- Transfer repo: POST /repos/{owner}/{repo}/transfer
  - Body: new_owner (required), new_name (optional), team_ids (org repos only); returns 202 and proceeds asynchronously. Source — https://docs.github.com/rest/repos/repos#transfer-a-repository

Collaborators and permissions
- Add collaborator: PUT /repos/{owner}/{repo}/collaborators/{username}
  - Request body permission (pull|triage|push|maintain|admin or custom role). 201 when a new invitation is created; updating an existing collaborator’s permission can return 204 (not always enumerated in responses). Outside collaborator invite limit: 50 per repo per 24h. Source — https://docs.github.com/rest/collaborators/collaborators#add-a-repository-collaborator
- List collaborators: GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/collaborators
  - Query affiliation (outside|direct|all), permission filter, per_page/page; response includes permissions object and role_name. Source — https://docs.github.com/rest/collaborators/collaborators#list-repository-collaborators
- Get collaborator permission: GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/collaborators/{username}/permission (route referenced by spec excerpt). Source — GitHub OpenAPI excerpt for this route

Branches and protection
- List branches: GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/branches
  - Query protected=true|false (filters); per_page default 30 (max 100); page. Response includes protection details. Source — https://docs.github.com/rest/branches/branches#list-branches
- Get branch protection: GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/branches/{branch}/protection
  - Response includes required_status_checks (contexts + checks), enforce_admins, required_pull_request_reviews (dismissal_restrictions, require_code_owner_reviews, required_approving_review_count in 0–6), etc. Source — https://docs.github.com/rest/branches/branch-protection#get-branch-protection
- Update branch protection: PUT /repos/{owner}/{repo}/branches/{branch}/protection
  - Constraints: combined total of users+apps+teams limited to 100; required_status_checks.contexts deprecated in favor of checks[] with context and app_id; org-only fields (e.g., restrictions, dismissal_restrictions). Source — https://docs.github.com/rest/branches/branch-protection#update-branch-protection
- Delete branch protection: DELETE /repos/{owner}/{repo}/branches/{branch}/protection (204 on success; 403 forbidden scenarios). Source — https://docs.github.com/rest/branches/branch-protection#delete-branch-protection

3) OpenAI endpoints (for context and parity)
