5) Security best practices tailored to each API (actionable)

GitHub
- Prefer GitHub App authentication for server-to-server use:
  - Create short‑lived installation access tokens (expire in 1 hour) and rotate them automatically before expiry; scope them to selected repositories (max 500) and minimal permissions needed for your task. Source — apps/create-installation-access-token
- Enforce repo governance via branch protection:
  - Use PUT /branches/{branch}/protection, favor checks[] over deprecated contexts, keep required_approving_review_count within 0–6, and watch the 100‑subject cap across users+teams+apps. Sources — repos/update-branch-protection, repos/get-branch-protection
- Handle rate limiting and pagination robustly:
  - Use GET /rate_limit (does not consume your limit) and honor Link headers and per_page/page parameters (per_page default 30, max 100 across many endpoints). Source — rate-limit/get; multiple repo endpoints
- Respect org policies and documented limits:
  - Inviting outside collaborators is limited to 50 per repo per 24h (expect 201 on create, possible 204 on permission updates). Deleting repos may be blocked by org policy (403) and requires delete_repo scope for OAuth/PAT. Sources — repos/add-collaborator; repos/delete
- Verify webhooks:
  - Check X-Hub-Signature-256 (HMAC SHA-256) and use the app webhook config secret (GET /app/hook/config). Sources — apps/get-webhook-config-for-app; x‑webhooks headers

OpenAI
- Treat API keys as high‑sensitivity credentials:
  - Store server-side only; rotate keys regularly; avoid client-side usage. Security in spec: ApiKeyAuth; server: https://api.openai.com/v1. Source — OpenAI OpenAPI overview
- Use the official endpoints and webhooks as documented:
