Tool `OpenAPI Explorer:getApiOperation` with Parameter {'id': 'github', 'operationIdOrRoute': 'repos/add-collaborator'} on OpenAPI Explorer succeeded. Result (summarized from 1389393 tokens): Summary of GitHub v3 REST API (OpenAPI 3.0.3) excerpt

API metadata
- Title: GitHub v3 REST API
- Version: 1.1.4
- Server: https://api.github.com
- Docs: https://docs.github.com/rest/
- License: MIT (https://spdx.org/licenses/MIT)
- Terms: https://docs.github.com/articles/github-terms-of-service
- Contact: https://support.github.com/contact?tags=dotcom-rest-api

Endpoint
- Method and path: PUT /repos/{owner}/{repo}/collaborators/{username}
- Summary: Add a repository collaborator
- Tag: repos
- Docs for this method: https://docs.github.com/rest/collaborators/collaborators#add-a-repository-collaborator

What it does
- Adds a user to a repository with a specified access level.
- For org-owned repositories, added users who aren’t org members become “outside collaborators” (or “repository collaborators” if they are Enterprise Managed Users, EMUs).
- Triggers GitHub notifications.
- Admin policies may restrict inviting outside collaborators (enterprise/org-level settings).
- Org base role restrictions: the granted permission must be equal to or higher than the organization’s base role for members; otherwise request fails with error: “Cannot assign {member} permission of {role name}”.
- If no request body is passed, set Content-Length to 0.
- Invitations:
  - Non-EMU users receive an invitation they must accept/decline (via notifications, email, or API).
  - EMUs are added automatically; no invitation is sent; EMUs can be added only within their enterprise.
- Updating existing collaborator’s permission: call the same endpoint with a different permission; if updated, response is 204 (no content), and there’s no other indication that it changed.

Rate limits
- Max 50 invitations per repository per 24 hours when inviting outside collaborators.
- No limit when inviting organization members to an org repository.

Parameters (path)
- owner (string, required): Account owner of the repo (case-insensitive).
- repo (string, required): Repo name without .git (case-insensitive).
- username (string, required): GitHub user handle.

Request body (application/json; optional)
- permission (string): Only valid for organization-owned repositories. Default: push.
  - Accepted values: pull, triage, push, maintain, admin, or a custom repository role name defined by the org.
- Example: { "permission": "triage" }

Responses
- 201 Created: A new invitation was created.
  - Body: Repository Invitation object, including:
    - id (integer): Unique identifier of the invitation.
    - repository: Minimal repository object (e.g., id, node_id, name, full_name, owner (Simple User), private, html_url, description, fork, url, various API URLs, permissions, role_name, visibility, timestamps, counts, booleans for features like issues/projects/wiki/pages/downloads/discussions, etc.). The owner is a Simple User object (e.g., login, id, avatar_url, urls, type, site_admin).
- 204 No Content: Indicates the permission for an existing collaborator was updated (no body).
  - Note: Although not explicitly listed in the responses block, the description states this is returned when updating permissions.
- Error cases: Not explicitly enumerated in this excerpt; standard GitHub API errors may apply.

Notes and references
- Outside collaborator management: https://docs.github.com/organizations/managing-user-access-to-your-organizations-repositories/managing-outside-collaborators/adding-outside-collaborators-to-repositories-in-your-organization
- Enterprise policy enforcement: https://docs.github.com/admin/policies/enforcing-policies-for-your-enterprise/enforcing-repository-management-policies-in-your-enterprise#enforcing-a-policy-for-inviting-outside-collaborators-to-repositories
- Org settings for adding outside collaborators: https://docs.github.com/organizations/managing-organization-settings/setting-permissions-for-adding-outside-collaborators
- Repository permission levels for organizations: https://docs.github.com/github/setting-up-and-managing-organizations-and-teams/reposit
