2025-12-01 10:01:54,532 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Below is a compact, calculation-ready framework you can plug your masses, volumes, humidity, and pressure into, plus worked numeric examples using standard, citable models. I also include the temperature conversions for your three readings and the core sources so your boss can verify everything.

1) Your temperature readings (converted)
- 156.7°F = 69.2778°C = 342.428 K
- 234.9°F = 112.7222°C = 385.872 K
- 89.3°F = 31.8333°C = 304.983 K
(Source: unit conversion)

2) Heating energy for materials (sensible heat)
Use Q = m · cp · ΔT, where:
- Q = heat (kJ), m = mass (kg), cp = specific heat (kJ/kg·K), ΔT in K or °C.
- Typical cp at ~20–30°C (verify against your specific alloy/material):
  - Aluminum: cp ≈ 0.897 kJ/kg·K
  - Carbon steel: cp ≈ 0.49 kJ/kg·K
  - Copper: cp ≈ 0.385 kJ/kg·K
  - Water: cp ≈ 4.186 kJ/kg·K
  Sources:
  - Copper/aluminum/water cp: NIST Chemistry WebBook; CRC Handbook; values summarized in Engineering Toolbox (Specific Heat of Solids)
  - Steel cp (varies by grade): ASM data; summarized in Engineering Toolbox

Worked example (assume baseline 20°C; replace with your actual start temp):
Target temps: 31.833°C, 69.278°C, 112.722°C
ΔT vs 20°C: 11.833 K; 49.278 K; 92.722 K

Per-kg sensible heat (kJ/kg):
- Aluminum (0.897): 10.62; 44.24; 83.18
- Carbon steel (0.49): 5.80; 24.15; 45.43
- Copper (0.385): 4.56; 18.96; 35.66
- Water (4.186): 49.53; 206.1; 388.0

To convert to kWh per metric tonne (1000 kg): multiply kJ/kg by 1000, then divide by 3600.
Example (steel to 69.278°C): 24,150 kJ/t ÷ 3600 = 6.7 kWh/t.
Notes:
- cp is temperature-dependent; if your ΔT is large, use average cp over the range (sources above give ranges or temperature-dependent data).
- These are ideal sensible heats; real process energy is higher due to losses/inefficiencies.

3) Material density from your mass and volume
ρmaterial = m / V (kg/m³)
- Typical densities for context (verify your specific grade):
  - Aluminum ≈ 2700 kg/m³
  - Carbon steel ≈ 7850 kg/m³
  - Copper ≈ 8960 kg/m³
  - HDPE ≈ 950 kg/m³
  Source: ASM/MatWeb; summarized in Engineering Toolbox

4) Air-side energy: temperature, humidity, pressure
Psychrometrics (ASHRAE model):
- Humidity ratio: W = 0.62198 · pv / (p − pv)
  where pv = φ · pws(T), φ = relative humidity (0–1), p = barometric pressure (kPa), pws(T) = saturation vapor pressure of water at T (kPa).
- Moist-air specific enthalpy (kJ per kg dry air):
  h = 1.006·T + W·(2501 + 1.86·T), with T in °C
- Moist air density at total pressure p (Pa) and temperature T (K):
  ρ = pd/(Rd·T) + pv/(Rv·T), with Rd = 287.058 J/kg·K, Rv = 461.495 J/kg·K, pd = p − pv
Key sources:
- ASHRAE Handbook—Fundamentals, Psychrometrics Chapter (standard industry equations)
- Saturation vapor pressure: Buck (1981), Journal of Applied Meteorology (widely used accurate equation for pws)

Worked example: heating ventilation air from 20°C, 50% RH to 30°C, 50% RH at sea level (p = 101.325 kPa)
- At 20°C: pws ≈ 2.338 kPa → pv = 1.169 kPa → W = 0.00726 kg/kgda
  h20 = 1.006·20 + 0.00726·(2501+1.86·20) ≈ 38.6 kJ/kg dry air
  ρ20 (moist) ≈ 1.199 kg/m³
- At 30°C: pws ≈ 4.241 kPa → pv = 2.121 kPa → W = 0.01329 kg/kgda
  h30 ≈ 64.2 kJ/kg dry air
- Enthalpy rise Δh ≈ 25.6 kJ/kg dry air
If airflow is 5000 m³/h at the 20°C state:
- Moist-air mass flow ≈ 5000 × 1.199 ≈ 5995 kg/h
- Dry-air mass flow ≈ 5995/(1+W20) ≈ 5952 kgda/h
- Heating load ≈ 5952 × 25.6 ≈ 152,000 kJ/h ≈ 42.3 kW (thermal)
Notes:
- If RH or pressure differs, recompute W, h, and ρ using the equations above.
- Latent heat for drying/dehumidification is large: the heat of vaporization varies approximately as Lv(T) ≈ 2501 − 2.381·T (kJ/kg, T in °C) per ASHRAE. Example: evaporating 10 kg water near 30°C needs ≈ 24.3 MJ minimum, plus sensible heats and losses.

Humidity and air density
- Warmer air is less dense; humidity further reduces density modestly. At 30°C, 0–100% RH changes density by ≈ −1.6%; 20→30°C at 50% RH changes density by ≈ −3.3% (sea level).
- This affects:
  - Fans at fixed speed/volume: motor power ∝ ρ (slightly lower power in hot/humid conditions for the same volumetric flow).
  - Systems controlling mass flow: speed increases to offset lower density, so power can rise.

5) Pressure effects and energy (fans, pumps, compressors)
Fans/pumps (affinity laws; DOE/AMCA/Hydraulic Institute):
- For geometric similarity and same fluid:
  - Flow Q ∝ speed N
  - Pressure rise Δp (or head H) ∝ N²
  - Shaft power P ∝ N³
- At a given speed, P ∝ ρ for the same system curve (denser fluid → more power). Pump hydraulic power P = ρ g Q H / η.
Sources: U.S. DOE “Improving Fan System Performance” and “Improving Pumping System Performance”; AMCA publications.

Compressors (isentropic model; Cengel & Boles; ASME PTC 10)
- Ideal isentropic specific work per kg:
  ws = (k/(k−1)) R T1 [ (p2/p1)^((k−1)/k) − 1 ]
  where T1 is inlet absolute temperature, p2/p1 is compression ratio, k is specific heat ratio (~1.4 for dry air), R = 287 J/kg·K.
Implications and example:
- Power scales roughly with T1 for fixed mass flow. From 293 K (20°C) to 305 K (32°C) at p2/p1 = 7:
  - ws(293 K) ≈ 219 kJ/kg; ws(305 K) ≈ 228 kJ/kg → ≈ +4.1% specific work.
- Pressure ratio is a strong driver: at 300 K, k=1.4:
  - r = 6 → ws ≈ 202 kJ/kg
  - r = 8 → ws ≈ 245 kJ/kg
  → ≈ +21% when going from 6:1 to 8:1.
Humidity effects on compression:
- Moisture slightly changes gas properties, but the dominant impact is via inlet density (hot/humid → lower density → less mass per intake stroke for positive-displacement compressors; to deliver the same mass flow, the compressor must run longer, increasing energy).
Sources: Cengel & Boles, Thermodynamics: An Engineering Approach; ASME PTC 10; DOE Compressed Air Sourcebook; vendor application notes (e.g., Atlas Copco, Ingersoll Rand) corroborate the temperature–power relationship qualitatively and quantitatively.

6) What I need from you to compute your exact energy numbers
Send:
- For each material batch: material type, mass (kg) or volume (m³), starting temperature (°C), target temperature(s) (°C). If only volume is known, I’ll compute m using ρ.
- For air systems: inlet/outlet temperatures (°C), RH (%), barometric pressure (kPa), airflow (m³/h or kg dry air/h), and whether the process controls volumetric or mass flow.
- For compressors/fans/pumps: inlet temperature and RH, suction/discharge pressures (or pressure ratio or head), controlled variable (mass flow, volumetric flow, speed), and rated efficiency.
With that, I’ll return plant-ready kW, kWh per batch, and sensitivity to T/RH/pressure with cited equations.

Key references (verifiable)
- ASHRAE Handbook—Fundamentals, Psychrometrics (enthalpy h = 1.006T + W(2501+1.86T), humidity ratio W = 0.62198 pv/(p−pv), moist air density model). ASHRAE, latest ed. Chapter 1. https://www.ashrae.org (chapter is standard and widely cited)
- Buck, A. L. (1981). New equations for computing vapor pressure and enhancement factor. Journal of Applied Meteorology, 20(12), 1527–1532. https://doi.org/10.1175/1520-0450(1981)020<1527:NEFCVP>2.0.CO;2
- Hyland, R. W., & Wexler, A. (1983). Formulations for the thermodynamic properties of dry air and moist air. ASHRAE Transactions 89(2A). (Foundational psychrometric formulation used by ASHRAE)
- Cengel, Y. A., & Boles, M. A. Thermodynamics: An Engineering Approach (isentropic compression work and compressor theory). McGraw-Hill.
- U.S. DOE, Advanced Manufacturing Office. Improving Fan System Performance: A Sourcebook for Industry. https://www.energy.gov/eere/amo/downloads/improving-fan-system-performance-sourcebook-industry
- U.S. DOE, Advanced Manufacturing Office. Improving Pumping System Performance: A Sourcebook for Industry. https://www.energy.gov/eere/amo/downloads/improving-pumping-system-performance-sourcebook-industry
- NIST Chemistry WebBook (material cp data; e.g., copper, aluminum); CRC Handbook; summarized values: Engineering Toolbox: https://www.engineeringtoolbox.com/
  - Specific heat of solids: https://www.engineeringtoolbox.com/specific-heat-solids-d_154.html
  - Water properties/latent heat: https://www.engineeringtoolbox.com/water-thermal-properties-d_162.html

If you share your actual masses, volumes, humidity, and pressure, I’ll compute your exact energy numbers (kW/kWh), enthalpy loads, and density-based impacts, with all calculations shown and source-backed.
