Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'Heat-related mortality in Europe during the summer of 2022 Nature Medicine 2023', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 1314 tokens): Overview
Three complementary studies quantify Europe’s recent heat-related mortality and the potential to reduce preventable deaths through adaptation and early warning.

2024 season and forecasting (Janoš et al., 2025; doi:10.1038/s41591-025-03954-7)
- Data and methods: New, harmonized daily mortality database from EARLY-ADAPT covering 654 contiguous regions in 32 European countries (entire urban and rural population of ~539 million). Epidemiological models assessed summers 2022–2024 and evaluated a continental, impact-based early warning system.
- Mortality in 2024: 62,775 heat-related deaths (95% CI 36,765–84,379).
- Comparison: 2024 exceeded 2023 (50,798; 29,442–68,610) but was below 2022 (67,873; 38,465–92,455) in this analysis.
- Forecast skill: Heat-health emergencies can be forecast with high confidence at least 1 week in advance, including for highly vulnerable regions and subgroups, enabling timely, reliable alerts for stakeholders to act and reduce preventable deaths.
- Implication: Adoption of impact-based, continental early warnings could materially strengthen public health response windows.

2023 season and role of adaptation (Gallo et al., 2024; doi:10.1038/s41591-024-03186-1)
- Data and methods: Temperature–mortality models for 823 contiguous regions in 35 countries (study period 2015–2023) to estimate sex- and age-specific heat-related mortality in 2023 and quantify mortality avoided by societal adaptation since 2000.
- Mortality in 2023: 47,690 deaths (95% CI 28,853–66,525), the second highest in 2015–2023, only surpassed by 2022. Note: The 2025 study above estimated 50,798 (29,442–68,610) for 2023, reflecting methodological/time-window differences.
- Adaptation effect: In the absence of present-century adaptation, 2023 heat-related mortality would have been +80.0% higher overall, and +100.7% higher among people aged 80+.
- Climate context: 2023 was the warmest year on record globally and the second warmest in Europe.
- Implication: Historical and ongoing adaptations have saved lives, but further, more effective strategies are urgently needed for hotter future summers.

2022 summer (Ballester et al., 2023; doi:10.1038/s41591-023-02419-z)
- Data and methods: Eurostat mortality database with 45,184,044 death counts from 823 contiguous regions across 35 European countries, representing >543 million people; period 30 May–4 September 2022.
- Mortality in 2022: 61,672 deaths (95% CI 37,643–86,807). Note: The 2025 study estimated 67,873 (38,465–92,455) for 2022, likely due to differing definitions/approaches.
- Country totals (highest): Italy 18,010 (13,793–22,225), Spain 11,324 (7,908–14,880), Germany 8,173 (5,374–11,018).
- Rates per million (highest): Italy 295 (226–364), Greece 280 (201–355), Spain 237 (166–312), Portugal 211 (162–255).
- Sex/age patterns: Relative to population, 56% more heat-related deaths in women than men; higher rates in men aged 0–64 (+41%) and 65–79 (+14%), and in women aged 80+ (+27%).
- Implication: Calls to reevaluate and strengthen heat surveillance, prevention plans, and long-term adaptation.

Cross-cutting insights
- Heat-related mortality remains very high across Europe, with 2024 exceeding 2023 and approaching 2022 levels.
- Estimates for 2022–2023 vary modestly between studies due to methodological and temporal differences but consistently rank 2022 highest and 2023 second highest in recent years.
- Adaptation since 2000 has substantially reduced mortality, particularly among the elderly, but significant residual risk persists.
- Impact-based, continental early warning systems show strong forecast skill at ≥1-week lead times, offering actionable windows for public health interventions to avert deaths. Links: PubMed 2025 (PMID: 40983745), 2024 (PMID: 39134730), 2023 (PMID: 37429922).
