- Paper ID: 39734426
  - Title: North Atlantic Heat Transport Convergence Derived from a Regional Energy Budget Using Different Ocean Heat Content Estimates.
  - Authors: Meyssignac B; Fourest S; Mayer M; Johnson GC; Calafat FM; Ablain M; Boyer T; Cheng L; Desbruyères D; Forget G; Giglio D; Kuusela M; Locarnini R; Lyman JM; Llovel W; Mishonov A; Reagan J; Rousseau V; Benveniste J
  - DOI: 10.1007/s10712-024-09865-5
  - Published date: 2024-01-01
  - Source/URL: PubMed — https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39734426/
  - Objective: Estimate North Atlantic ocean heat transport convergence (OHTC) for 2005–2018 using an ocean energy budget and validate against in-situ array measurements.
  - Methods:
    - Horizontal OHT convergence inferred from the tendency of ocean heat content (OHC) primarily derived from satellite altimetry combined with space gravimetry.
    - Net surface energy fluxes derived from mass-corrected divergence of atmospheric energy transport and tendency from ECMWF ERA5, combined with top-of-atmosphere radiative fluxes from CERES.
    - Spatial domain bounded by the RAPID section at 26.5°N (operational since 2004) and the OSNAP section at 53°–60°N (operational since 2014).
    - Cross-validation with independent OHT estimates from RAPID and OSNAP in-situ observations.
    - Sensitivity tested using multiple OHC estimation methods; robustness assessed by correlation strength despite spread among methods.
  - Key results:
    - Mean North Atlantic ocean energy budget between RAPID and OSNAP closes to within ±0.25 PW.
    - Mean OHT convergence between these sections: 0.58 ± 0.25 PW, in good agreement with section-based observations.
    - Interannual variability agrees reasonably with RAPID/OSNAP: correlation 0.54 (annual series); improves to 0.67 for biannual series.
    - Similar results obtained across alternative OHC methods; correlations remain significant, indicating robustness.
  - Implications: A regional energy budget approach using satellite/gravity OHC tendencies and reanalysis/TOA fluxes can reliably reproduce mean and interannual North Atlantic OHT convergence consistent with in-situ arrays for 2005–2018.
