- Overall: Canada’s 2023 wildfire season was unprecedented in scale, intensity, and impacts. Multiple studies document record area burned, exceptional emissions, major contributions to global tree cover loss, severe air quality effects in Canada and the northeastern US, and a strong human-driven climate influence that increases the likelihood of such extremes. Related Arctic research highlights aerosol patterns and trends that affect Arctic amplification.

Key findings by study

1) Human driven climate change increased the likelihood of the 2023 record area burned in Canada. (Kirchmeier-Young et al., 2024; doi:10.1038/s41612-024-00841-9)
- Scope/impacts: ~15 million hectares burned in 2023—more than double the previous Canadian record—triggering record evacuations, unprecedented air-quality impacts across Canada and the northeastern US, and straining fire management capacity.
- Attribution: Climate-model analyses show human-induced climate change significantly increased the likelihood of 2023-scale area burned across most of Canada; increases exceed two-fold in eastern and southwestern Canada.
- Seasonality and fire weather: The unusually long 2023 fire season was >5 times as likely due to human influence. Large, synchronous regions of extreme fire weather across Canada were also much more likely under human influence.
- Emissions: Simulated wildfire emissions in 2023 were ~8 times the 1985–2022 mean.
- Outlook: Continued warming is projected to further increase the likelihood of extreme fire seasons and associated health, societal, and ecosystem impacts.
- Source: PubMed (published 2024).

2) Carbon emissions from the 2023 Canadian wildfires. (Byrne et al., 2024; doi:10.1038/s41586-024-07878-z)
- Scale: The 2023 fires were extreme in scale and intensity, with >7 times the average annual area burned relative to the previous four decades.
- Focus: Quantifies carbon emissions from the 2023 season.
- Source: PubMed (published 2024).

3) Extreme wildfires in Canada and their contribution to global loss in tree cover and carbon emissions in 2023. (MacCarthy et al., 2024; doi:10.1111/gcb.17392)
- Forest impacts: ~7.8 million hectares of forest burned in 2023, accounting for >25% of global tree-cover loss that year.
- Emissions: Forests affected by the fires emitted nearly 3 billion tons of CO2.
- Context: Emphasizes the role of extreme heat and low rainfall (linked to climate change) in driving these unprecedented fires.
- Source: PubMed (published 2024).

4) Sources, variability, long-term trends, and radiative forcing of aerosols in the Arctic: implications for Arctic amplification. (Kuttippurath et al., 2024; doi:10.1007/s11356-023-31245-6)
- Dataset/period: Ground-based, satellite, and reanalysis data (2003–2019) on aerosol optical depth (AOD), black carbon (BC), and dust across the Arctic.
- Spatial patterns: Lowest aerosol levels in Greenland and the Central Arctic; higher levels over northern Eurasia and parts of North America.
- Seasonality: All aerosol types peak in spring; Arctic haze is prominent.
- Trends/forcing: Significant positive AOD trends and aerosol radiative forcing with implications for Arctic amplification (regional warming feedbacks).
- Source: PubMed (published 2024).

Synthesis/implications
- The 2023 Canadian wildfire season set national records for area burned and drove exceptional smoke and air-quality impacts regionally and internationally.
- Human-caused climate change substantially increased the probability of the extreme fire weather, longer season length, and 2023-scale burned area, especially in eastern and southwestern Canada.
- Emissions and land-cover impacts were global in significance: ~7.8 Mha forest burned (>25% of global tree-cover loss in 2023) and ~3 Gt CO2 emitted; total Canadian area burned reached ~15 Mha.
