Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'Vicedo-Cabrera 2021 heat-related mortality Nature Climate Change', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result: [{"paper_id":"34221128","title":"The burden of heat-related mortality attributable to recent human-induced climate change.","authors":"Vicedo-Cabrera AM; Scovronick N; Sera F; Royé D; Schneider R; Tobias A; Astrom C; Guo Y; Honda Y; Hondula DM; Abrutzky R; Tong S; de Sousa Zanotti Stagliorio Coelho M; Saldiva PHN; Lavigne E; Correa PM; Ortega NV; Kan H; Osorio S; Kyselý J; Urban A; Orru H; Indermitte E; Jaakkola JJK; Ryti N; Pascal M; Schneider A; Katsouyanni K; Samoli E; Mayvaneh F; Entezari A; Goodman P; Zeka A; Michelozzi P; de'Donato F; Hashizume M; Alahmad B; Diaz MH; De La Cruz Valencia C; Overcenco A; Houthuijs D; Ameling C; Rao S; Ruscio FD; Carrasco-Escobar G; Seposo X; Silva S; Madureira J; Holobaca IH; Fratianni S; Acquaotta F; Kim H; Lee W; Iniguez C; Forsberg B; Ragettli MS; Guo YLL; Chen BY; Li S; Armstrong B; Aleman A; Zanobetti A; Schwartz J; Dang TN; Dung DV; Gillett N; Haines A; Mengel M; Huber V; Gasparrini A","abstract":"Climate change affects human health; however, there have been no large-scale, systematic efforts to quantify the heat-related human health impacts that have already occurred due to climate change. Here, we use empirical data from 732 locations in 43 countries to estimate the mortality burdens associated with the additional heat exposure that has resulted from recent human-induced warming, during the period 1991-2018. Across all study countries, we find that 37.0% (range 20.5-76.3%) of warm-season heat-related deaths can be attributed to anthropogenic climate change and that increased mortality is evident on every continent. Burdens varied geographically but were of the order of dozens to hundreds of deaths per year in many locations. Our findings support the urgent need for more ambitious mitigation and adaptation strategies to minimize the public health impacts of climate change.","doi":"10.1038/s41558-021-01058-x","published_date":"2021-01-01T00:00:00","pdf_url":"","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34221128/","source":"pubmed","updated_date":"2021-01-01T00:00:00","categories":"","keywords":"","citations":0,"references":"","extra":""}]
Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'Record North Atlantic sea surface temperature 2023 Nature Communications', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 1205 tokens): - Paper ID: 41315212
  - Title: Concurrent warming, freshening and cessation of deep convection in the Labrador Sea raised its sea level to a record high.
  - Authors: Yashayaev I; Zhang Y
  - Source/DOI/Date: Nature Communications; doi:10.1038/s41467-025-65747-3; 2025-01-01. PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41315212/
  - Context: The Labrador Sea is a key North Atlantic carbon sink and source of ventilated intermediate waters; since the 1950s it has undergone some of the largest full-depth temperature and salinity changes observed.
  - Methods: Satellite altimetry combined with Argo float profiles and ship-based hydrography.
  - Findings (2017–2025): Exceptionally rapid sea level rise to a record high in the central Labrador Sea driven by six concurrent factors:
    1) Reduced winter cooling
    2) Enhanced summer warming
    3) Anomalous freshening
    4) Cessation of deep convection
    5) Reduced deep-water density
    6) Water-column mass gain
  - Mechanisms: Temperature-driven sea level changes controlled by surface heat fluxes. Salinity effects shifted from counterbalancing temperature (1948–2015) to reinforcing it (2015–2023). Unprecedented freshening linked to extreme Arctic sea-ice losses with a ~2-year lag, making these losses essential contributors to the 2017–2025 sea level rise.

- Paper ID: 40670333
  - Title: The longest-lasting 2023 western North American heat wave was fueled by the record-warm Atlantic Ocean.
  - Authors: Lopez H; Lee SK; West R; Kim D; Jia L
  - Source/DOI/Date: Nature Communications; doi:10.1038/s41467-025-61859-y; 2025-01-01. PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40670333/
  - Context: WMO ranked 2023 as the second warmest year since 1850, with record warm land-surface temperatures over >20% of global land. The southwestern US and northern Mexico saw their longest stretch of record-breaking heat, affecting >100 million people, causing >200 deaths, and ~$14.5B in losses.
  - Methods: Regression analysis and atmospheric model simulations.
  - Findings: A strong anticyclonic blocking pattern persisted >6 weeks across the western US and was ultimately forced by extremely warm Atlantic SSTs. The combination of a warm Atlantic and a developing Pacific El Niño substantially amplified regional heat waves—doubling their number, tripling total heat-wave days, and increasing event duration by ~50%.

- Paper ID: 37164947
  - Title: Moist and warm conditions in Eurasia during the last glacial of the Middle Pleistocene Transition.
  - Authors: Sánchez Goñi MF; Extier T; Polanco-Martínez JM; Zorzi C; Rodrigues T; Bahr A
  - Source/DOI/Date: Nature Communications; doi:10.1038/s41467-023-38337-4; 2023. PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37164947/
  - Context: The end of the Middle Pleistocene Transition (MPT; ~800–670 ka) saw emergence of large glacial ice sheets alongside anomalously warm North Atlantic SSTs that enhanced moisture production, but moisture transport pathways across Eurasia remained uncertain.
  - Data/Methods: Combined proxy records of upper-ocean temperature and pollen-based Mediterranean forest cover (a tracer of westerlies/precipitation) from a subtropical drill core off SW Iberia with records of East Asian Summer Monsoon (EASM) strength, West Pacific SSTs, and climate model simulations.
  - Findings: During Marine Isotope Stage 18 (MIS 18) glacial, southwestern European winter precipitation and EASM strength reached high levels. Nearly continuous moisture supply from both the Atlantic and Pacific, transported to higher latitudes by strengthened westerlies, likely fueled accelerated expansion of Northern Hemisphere ice sheets during the late MPT.
Tool `Paper Search:search_arxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'Earth heat inventory 2023 von Schuckmann Earth System Science Data', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 4636 tokens): - Video-R2: Reinforcing Consistent and Grounded Reasoning in Multimodal Language Models (Maaz et al.)
  - Problem: Video reasoning traces in MLLMs can be logically inconsistent or weakly grounded in visuals.
  - Diagnostics: Introduces Think Answer Consistency (TAC) for reasoning–answer alignment and Video Attention Score (VAS) for visual vs. textual reliance; analysis across 11 video reasoning benchmarks shows heavy reliance on linguistic priors.
  - Method: Timestamp-aware supervised fine-tuning + Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) with a Temporal Alignment Reward (TAR) to enforce temporally aligned, causally coherent reasoning.
  - Results: Consistently higher TAC, VAS, and accuracy across multiple benchmarks; code/dataset/model will be open-sourced.

- Video-CoM: Interactive Video Reasoning via Chain of Manipulations (Rasheed et al.)
  - Paradigm: “Think with videos” via a Chain of Manipulations (CoM) that iteratively performs visual actions to gather/verify evidence, overcoming the passive “encode-once, reason-in-text” bottleneck.
  - Data/Training: Video-CoM Instruct (18K multi-step manipulation instructions); reinforcement learning with reasoning-aware GRPO using step-level reasoning rewards (not just sparse answer rewards).
  - Results: Strong performance on 9 video reasoning benchmarks, +3.6% average over recent SOTA while using only 25K SFT and 3K GRPO video samples; ablations show improved accuracy and interpretability.
  - Code: https://github.com/mbzuai-oryx/Video-CoM

- Thinking by Doing: Building Efficient World Model Reasoning in LLMs via Multi-turn Interaction (Shu et al.)
  - Goal: Enable LLM agents to internalize world models efficiently via active interaction rather than rigid reasoning templates.
  - WMAct mechanisms: (1) Reward rescaling by action efficacy to reduce redundancy and promote purposeful interaction; (2) Interaction frequency annealing to progressively limit turns, forcing internalization.
  - Results: On Sokoban, Maze, and Taxi, solves tasks in a single turn that previously needed multiple interactions; strong transferability and gains on broader reasoning benchmarks.

- AnyTalker: Scaling Multi-Person Talking Video Generation with Interactivity Refinement (Zhong et al.)
  - Framework: Extensible multi-stream architecture with an identity-aware attention mechanism in a Diffusion Transformer to iteratively process identity–audio pairs, scaling to many identities.
  - Training: Primarily from single-person videos to learn multi-person speaking patterns, with limited real multi-person clips for interactivity refinement.
  - Contribution: New evaluation metric and dataset for naturalness/interactivity.
  - Results: Strong lip sync, visual quality, and natural interactivity, balancing data cost and identity scalability.

- A Lyapunov-Based Small-Gain Theorem for Fixed-Time Stability (Tang, Krstic, Poveda)
  - Theory: New Lyapunov-based small-gain methodology for fixed-time stability (FxTS) in interconnected systems.
  - Assumptions: Each subsystem admits a fixed-time input-to-state stability (FxT-ISS) Lyapunov function; a nonlinear small-gain condition ensures the full interconnection is FxTS.
  - Significance: Complements existing small-gain results for asymptotic/finite-time stability; bridges a gap for FxTS.
  - Examples: Analytical/numerical illustrations, including fixed-time feedback optimization without time-scale separation.

- ThetaEvolve: Test-time Learning on Open Problems (Wang et al.)
  - Framework: Open-source evolution framework that scales in-context learning and test-time reinforcement learning (RL) for improving bounds on open optimization problems.
  - Design: Single-LLM system, large program database, batch sampling, lazy penalties to discourage stagnation, optional reward shaping for stable signals.
  - Results: Small open-source model (DeepSeek-R1-0528-Qwen3-8B) achieves new best-known bounds on circle packing and the first auto-correlation inequality; across two model


