Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'Another Year of Record Heat for the Oceans 2023 Advances in Atmospheric Sciences', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result: [{"paper_id":"36643611","title":"Another Year of Record Heat for the Oceans.","authors":"Cheng L; Abraham J; Trenberth KE; Fasullo J; Boyer T; Mann ME; Zhu J; Wang F; Locarnini R; Li Y; Zhang B; Yu F; Wan L; Chen X; Feng L; Song X; Liu Y; Reseghetti F; Simoncelli S; Gouretski V; Chen G; Mishonov A; Reagan J; Li G","abstract":"Changes in ocean heat content (OHC), salinity, and stratification provide critical indicators for changes in Earth's energy and water cycles. These cycles have been profoundly altered due to the emission of greenhouse gasses and other anthropogenic substances by human activities, driving pervasive changes in Earth's climate system. In 2022, the world's oceans, as given by OHC, were again the hottest in the historical record and exceeded the previous 2021 record maximum. According to IAP/CAS data, the 0-2000 m OHC in 2022 exceeded that of 2021 by 10.9 ± 8.3 ZJ (1 Zetta Joules = 10","doi":"10.1007/s00376-023-2385-2","published_date":"2023-01-01T00:00:00","pdf_url":"","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36643611/","source":"pubmed","updated_date":"2023-01-01T00:00:00","categories":"","keywords":"","citations":0,"references":"","extra":""}]
