Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'Ballester heat related mortality Europe 2022 Nature Medicine 2023', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 1314 tokens): Key findings across Europe’s 2022–2024 heat seasons and implications for health protection

Study scope and methods
- 2022 analysis: Eurostat mortality data from 823 contiguous regions across 35 European countries, covering >543 million people; period 30 May–4 Sept 2022; epidemiological models.
- 2023 analysis: Temperature and mortality for 2015–2023 in the same 823 regions/35 countries; sex- and age-specific estimates; epidemiological models.
- 2024 analysis: Newly created format-homogeneous daily mortality database from EARLY-ADAPT, covering 654 contiguous regions in 32 countries (entire urban and rural population of 539 million); epidemiological models plus evaluation of a new generation of continental impact-based early warning systems.

Heat-related mortality burden
- 2022 (record hottest European summer):
  - Estimated heat-related deaths: 61,672 (95% CI 37,643–86,807) between 30 May and 4 Sept 2022.
  - Countries with highest numbers: Italy 18,010 (13,793–22,225); Spain 11,324 (7,908–14,880); Germany 8,173 (5,374–11,018).
  - Highest mortality rates (per million): Italy 295 (226–364); Greece 280 (201–355); Spain 237 (166–312); Portugal 211 (162–255).
  - Demographics: Relative to population, 56% more heat-related deaths in women than men; higher rates observed in men aged 0–64 (+41%) and 65–79 (+14%), and in women aged 80+ (+27%).
- 2023 (warmest year globally; second warmest in Europe):
  - Study A (823 regions/35 countries): 47,690 (28,853–66,525) heat-related deaths in 2023—second highest in 2015–2023, only surpassed by 2022.
  - Study B (EARLY-ADAPT comparison figures): 50,798 (29,442–68,610) heat-related deaths in 2023.
- 2024:
  - Estimated heat-related deaths: 62,775 (36,765–84,379).
  - Contextual comparison (EARLY-ADAPT): 2024 exceeded 2023 (50,798; 29,442–68,610) but was somewhat below 2022 (67,873; 38,465–92,455).
  - Note: Differences in 2022/2023 estimates reflect differing datasets/coverage (823 regions/35 countries vs 654 regions/32 countries and databases).

Adaptation and avoided mortality (2000–2023)
- The 2023 analysis estimates the heat-related mortality burden in 2023 would have been +80.0% higher without societal adaptation since 2000.
- The protective effect is especially pronounced among the elderly: +100.7% higher in people aged 80+ in the absence of adaptation.
- Findings underscore the life-saving role of historical and ongoing adaptation measures, while highlighting the urgency to strengthen strategies as summers get hotter.

Early warning and forecasting (2024 study)
- A new generation of impact-based, continental-scale early warning systems can forecast heat-related health emergencies with high confidence at least 1 week in advance.
- Forecast skill holds even for highly vulnerable regions and population subgroups.
- Implication: Adopting such systems would enable reliable heat-health emergency alerts within decision-relevant lead times, supporting timely interventions to reduce preventable deaths.

Overall implications
- Successive record-breaking summers in Europe (2022–2024) produced very high heat-related mortality, with 2024 again imposing a substantial burden.
- Country-level and demographic disparities persist, with notable vulnerability among older adults and country-specific burdens (e.g., Italy, Spain, Germany).
- Strengthening heat surveillance, prevention plans, and long-term adaptation—combined with operational early warning systems—can materially reduce future heat-related mortality.
