Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': '10.1029/2021GL093047', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 4230 tokens): Here are concise summaries of each item, preserving key aims, methods, findings, and implications (noting where abstracts were truncated in the provided content):

- Comparative evaluation of impact-based and hybrid approaches for real time earthquake shaking prediction (Rea et al., 2025; doi:10.1038/s41598-025-17889-z)
  - Compares two Earthquake Early Warning (EEW) algorithms: PLUM (pure impact-based) and QuakeUp (hybrid, P-wave shaking forecast based).
  - Evaluates how quickly and accurately they forecast ground motion using real-time data by simulating a 2016 earthquake event (abstract truncated; specific event magnitude/detail not fully available).
  - Focus: lead time vs. accuracy tradeoffs between impact-based and hybrid methods in real-time shaking prediction.

- Relation between magnetopause position and reconnection rate under quasi-steady solar wind dynamic pressure (Kim et al., 2024; doi:10.1186/s40623-024-02101-9)
  - Uses SuperDARN observations and empirical magnetopause models (Shue 1998; Lin 2010) for three cases with IMF rotations under quasi-steady solar wind dynamic pressure.
  - Reconnection rate estimated via electric field along the open-closed boundary in its moving frame; magnetopause position near local noon estimated from OMNI-driven models.
  - Finds anti-correlation: reconnection rate increases as magnetopause moves Earthward; also increases with empirical solar wind–magnetosphere coupling efficiency.

- Increasingly Sophisticated Climate Models Need the Out-Of-Sample Tests Paleoclimates Provide (Burls & Sagoo, 2022; doi:10.1029/2022MS003389)
  - Perspective on growing complexity in climate models (e.g., cloud microphysics) increasing degrees of freedom and spread in climate sensitivity (notably in CMIP6).
  - Historical constraints are limited due to ocean heat uptake and disequilibrium.
  - Advocates confronting models with paleoclimate “out-of-sample” tests (e.g., Zhu et al., 2022) during development to better constrain global climate sensitivity and regional patterns (polar amplification, subtropical aridification).

- Global Distribution of Key Features of Streamer Corona Discharges in Thunderclouds (Soler et al., 2022; doi:10.1029/2022JD037535)
  - Presents nighttime global distributions of BLUEs (Blue LUminous Events) detected by ASIM MMIA.
  - ~10% show an impulsive single-pulse shape; others are unclear single, multiple, or ambiguous pulses.
  - Identifies two distinct populations by peak power density (<25 µW m−…; abstract truncated).

- Low Frequency ULF Waves in the Earth's Inner Magnetosphere: Power Spectra During High Speed Streams and Quiet Solar Wind and Seeding of EMIC Waves (Gamayunov & Engebretson, 2022; doi:10.1029/2022JA030647)
  - Extends prior CME-focused work to high-speed streams (HSS) and quiet solar wind (QSW).
  - Key results:
    - Transverse and compressional magnetic field power spectra follow power laws (~mHz–Hz); CME and HSS parameters are similar, QSW differs.
    - During 0 < SYM/H ≤ 25 nT, compressional power dominating transverse suggests HSS; the opposite suggests CMEs.
    - Regardless of driving, a turbulent cascade supplies nonthermal seed fluctuations necessary for EMIC wave growth (~Hz).

- Magnetosphere-Ionosphere-Thermosphere Coupling Study at Jupiter Based on Juno's First 30 Orbits and Modeling Tools (Al Saati et al., 2022; doi:10.1029/2022JA030586)
  - Extends Wang et al. (2021) method using Juno multi-instrument data plus modeling to retrieve ionospheric conductances, FACs, horizontal currents, electric fields, precipitating electron fluxes, and particle/Joule heating along Juno’s footprints, over the first 30 science orbits in both hemispheres.
  - Finds large orbit-to-orbit and hemispheric variability with dominant trends: southern current systems consistent with sub-corotating ionospheric plasma; north exhibits both super- and sub-corotating flows.
  - Assesses implications for momentum/energy transfer between upper atmosphere and equatorial magnet
