Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': '10.5194/essd-12-1561-2020', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 4397 tokens): - 1064 nm rotational Raman polarization lidar for profiling aerosol and cloud characteristics (Wang et al., 2024, doi:10.1364/OE.518259)
  - Developed a novel 1064 nm lidar (Wuhan University) using rotational Raman detection centered at 1056 nm (6 nm bandwidth) to boost SNR and reduce temperature sensitivity.
  - Split elastic polarization channels (1064 nm parallel and cross) into near- and far-range paths to extend dynamic range.
  - Used silicon SPADs in photon-counting mode (QE ≈ 2.5%); 3 W DPSS Nd:YAG laser; 250 mm Cassegrain telescope.
  - Detectable range: ~0.3 km to 12–15 km.
  - Demonstrated retrievals at 1064 nm of backscatter, extinction, lidar ratio, and depolarization for aerosols and mixed-phase clouds.

- The development of deep-ocean anoxia in a comprehensive ocean phosphorus model (Donohue et al., 2023, doi:10.1007/s13137-023-00221-0)
  - Analytic treatment of the 4-box ocean phosphorus model (Slomp & Van Cappellen, 2007) including coupled water, carbon, and oxygen cycles.
  - Shows all reservoir C and P concentrations are slaved to deep-ocean soluble reactive phosphorus (SRP), which relaxes to equilibrium on ~180 kyr.
  - Identifies a critical parameter determining oxic vs anoxic deep-ocean states and derives it explicitly; explores dependence on physical parameters.
