- Ganoderma lucidum polysaccharides protect retinal neurovascular units in rat retinal ischemia–reperfusion injury (DOI: 10.1038/s41598-025-26957-3)
  - Model: Postnatal day-6 rats; 70 mmHg anterior chamber perfusion for 1 h; daily GLP gavage (35, 70, 140 mg/kg) for 7 days.
  - Outcomes:
    - Structure/function: Reduced retinal thinning and apoptosis (H&E, TUNEL); improved ERG a-/b-waves and PhNR.
    - Oxidative stress: Upregulated Nrf2/HO-1, increased SOD activity, decreased MDA (stronger at 140 mg/kg).
    - Glial/immune: Decreased GFAP and IBA-1 (less reactive astrogliosis and microglial activation).
    - Vascular: Less tortuosity, higher capillary perfusion (OCTA); downregulated VEGF, HIF-1α, Notch; upregulated VE-cadherin and eNOS mRNA.
    - NVU coupling: Modulated Cx43 and AQP4, indicating restored intercellular communication.
  - Conclusion: GLP mitigates oxidative stress, gliosis, and neovascularization; stabilizes endothelial junctions; and preserves retinal electrophysiology and perfusion.

- Dual-responsive bis-Schiff base fluorescent probe for simultaneous detection of Zn2+ (DOI: 10.1038/s41598-025-26717-3)
