 FINAL SOLUTION:
2025-12-01 11:14:03,144 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Executive summary (what matters most)
- The specific BRAF mutation does matter for outcomes in melanoma.
- Among V600 variants, V600K behaves differently from V600E: V600K generally responds less well to BRAF/MEK–targeted therapy but tends to respond better to PD‑1–based immunotherapy. Multiple datasets support this with concrete response-rate and time‑to‑event differences.
- Non‑V600 BRAF mutations (class II and class III) generally do not benefit from standard BRAF inhibitors. Some class II mutations (for example K601E, L597Q/R) show meaningful—but lower and less consistent—activity with MEK inhibitors; class III mutations (for example D594, G466) rarely respond to BRAF/MEK therapy and are best managed with immunotherapy and/or clinical trials (for example, ERK or pan‑RAF inhibitors).

Key evidence with specific numbers and sources
1) Targeted therapy in BRAF V600E vs V600K
- Dabrafenib + trametinib (COMBI‑d and COMBI‑v pooled analysis; metastatic setting)
  - Overall results: objective response rate (ORR) ~68–69%; median progression‑free survival (PFS) ~11 months; median overall survival (OS) ~25–26 months; 5‑year OS ~34%.
  - Subgroup by mutation: patients with V600K had consistently lower response and shorter PFS than those with V600E (for example, lower ORR by roughly 10–20 percentage points and shorter PFS by several months). These differences persist across analyses.
  - Sources:
    • Robert C et al. Five‑Year Outcomes with Dabrafenib plus Trametinib in Metastatic Melanoma. N Engl J Med. 2019. ClinicalTrials.gov: NCT01584648, NCT01597908.
    • Long GV et al. Combined BRAF and MEK inhibition versus BRAF inhibition alone in melanoma: pooled long‑term analysis of COMBI‑d/v. (Long‑term pooled analyses reported in Lancet Oncology/Ann Oncol; see COMBI‑d/v updates for mutation‑specific subgroup data.)
- Encorafenib + binimetinib (COLUMBUS; metastatic setting)
  - Overall results: ORR ~63–70%; median PFS 14.9 months; median OS 33.6 months; durable tail on survival curves in a subset.
  - Subgroup by mutation: V600K patients again had lower response and shorter PFS versus V600E in exploratory analyses.
  - Sources:
    • Dummer R et al. Encorafenib plus binimetinib versus vemurafenib or encorafenib in BRAF‑mutant melanoma (COLUMBUS). Lancet Oncol. 2018; NCT01909453.
    • Long‑term updates (Lancet Oncol/JCO 2021–2023) report sustained OS benefit; subgroup trends by V600E/K are consistent with earlier reports.

2) Immunotherapy in BRAF V600E vs V600K
- Large multicenter observational cohort directly comparing outcomes by V600 subtype on modern immunotherapy and targeted therapy:
  - Anti–PD‑1 therapy (pembrolizumab or nivolumab, ± ipilimumab): ORR 53% in V600K vs 35% in V600E; PFS and OS favored V600K.
  - Targeted therapy (BRAF ± MEK inhibitors): ORR 36% in V600K vs 58% in V600E; PFS and OS favored V600E.
  - These data align with the biological observation that V600K melanomas often have higher UV‑induced tumor mutational burden (TMB), which may enhance immunotherapy responsiveness, while V600E tumors are more “oncogene‑addicted” to MAPK signaling, favoring targeted therapy.
  - Source:
    • Pires da Silva I, Menzies AM, et al. Distinct outcomes to immunotherapy and targeted therapy for V600K versus V600E BRAF‑mutant melanoma. Journal of Clinical Oncology. 2020. (Multicenter retrospective; provides the ORR 53% vs 35% for anti–PD‑1 and 36% vs 58% for BRAFi/MEKi.)

3) Non‑V600 BRAF mutations (class II and class III)
- Classification (functional):
  - Class I (V600): high kinase activity, RAS‑independent monomers (respond to BRAF ± MEK inhibitors).
  - Class II (for example K601E, L597Q/R, G469A/V): intermediate/high kinase activity, RAS‑independent dimers (often insensitive to first‑generation BRAF inhibitors; MEK inhibitors can have activity).
  - Class III (for example D594, G466, N581): kinase‑impaired; signal via RAS/CRAF; typically not responsive to BRAF inhibitors; MEK/ERK inhibition may help in selected cases; immunotherapy remains foundational.
- Clinical activity with MEK inhibition in non‑V600:
  - Multiple small series and basket trials show objective responses in class II mutations—commonly in the 20–40% range—with trametinib or other MEK inhibitors; median PFS is typically limited (approximately 3–9 months), and benefit is mutation‑dependent (K601E and L597Q/R among the more responsive).
  - Class III mutations rarely respond to MEK monotherapy; when benefit occurs it may be short‑lived. Pan‑RAF or ERK inhibitors (or combinations) are under study.
  - Sources:
    • Menzer C et al. Targeted therapy in advanced melanoma with non‑V600 BRAF mutations: outcomes with MEK inhibition. Eur J Cancer. 2019. (Multi‑institutional cohort; reports responses concentrated in class II, sparse in class III, with PFS generally months.)
    • Dankner M et al. BRAF kinase domain mutations confer variable sensitivity to MAPK pathway inhibitors. Clin Cancer Res. 2018. (Preclinical/clinical correlation; details mutation‑specific drug sensitivity across class II/III.)
    • Yao Z et al. Tumours with class 3 BRAF mutants are sensitive to the inhibition of activated RAS. Nature. 2017 / Sci Transl Med. 2017. (Mechanistic data explaining class II/III behavior; supports trial strategies beyond first‑gen BRAF inhibitors.)
    • Early‑phase trials of ERK or pan‑RAF inhibitors (for example, ulixertinib, lifirafenib, belvarafenib) report signals of activity in class II/III BRAF melanomas; confirmatory data are evolving.

4) Adjuvant setting (resected stage III V600E/K)
- Dabrafenib + trametinib (COMBI‑AD): significant and durable relapse‑free survival (RFS) benefit versus placebo; 5‑year analyses show sustained RFS and emerging OS benefit. Subgroup analyses indicate benefit in both V600E and V600K; no validated signal that one subtype loses benefit.
  - Source:
    • Long GV et al. Adjuvant Dabrafenib plus Trametinib in Stage III BRAF‑Mutated Melanoma (COMBI‑AD). N Engl J Med. 2017; with 5‑year update (2020–2022). NCT01682083.

5) Sequencing therapy when V600 is present
- Randomized sequencing data favor starting with immunotherapy for fit patients, reserving BRAF/MEK for later, regardless of V600E vs V600K. This aligns particularly well with the stronger immunotherapy performance seen in V600K.
  - DREAMseq (EA6134): nivolumab + ipilimumab first, then dabrafenib + trametinib at progression, achieved superior 2‑year OS (72%) versus the reverse sequence (52%).
  - Sources:
    • Atkins MB et al. DREAMseq (EA6134). J Clin Oncol. 2023. NCT02224781.
    • SECOMBIT trial (phase II) reports concordant results.

What this means in practice (mutation‑specific implications)
- BRAF V600E
  - Targeted therapy: high and rapid response rates (ORR roughly two‑thirds; median PFS ~11–15 months depending on regimen).
  - Immunotherapy: strong long‑term benefit rates; choice between immunotherapy first vs targeted first should consider disease tempo and burden; randomized sequencing trials favor immunotherapy first for eligible patients.
- BRAF V600K
  - Targeted therapy: lower responses and shorter PFS than V600E.
  - Immunotherapy: higher ORR and better PFS/OS compared with V600E in retrospective multicenter data; immunotherapy is especially attractive as first‑line when clinically feasible.
- BRAF non‑V600 (class II/III)
  - Do not use single‑agent BRAF inhibitors.
  - Consider MEK inhibitor therapy for class II mutations (for example K601E, L597Q/R), recognizing ORR is typically in the 20–40% range and median PFS usually months; clinical trials (for example ERK or pan‑RAF ± MEK combinations) are encouraged.
  - Immunotherapy remains a mainstay across classes, given the limitations of targeted options in class II/III.

Additional source notes you can verify quickly
- COMBI‑d/v: NCT01584648, NCT01597908; pooled 5‑year outcomes paper in N Engl J Med (2019) and prior pooled analyses detail V600E vs V600K subgroup differences.
- COLUMBUS: NCT01909453; Dummer R et al., Lancet Oncology 2018; long‑term updates detail durability and subgroup trends.
- COMBI‑AD: NCT01682083; NEJM 2017 with multi‑year updates; benefit consistent in V600E and V600K.
- DREAMseq: NCT02224781; JCO 2023 final OS.
- V600K vs V600E immunotherapy/targeted comparisons: Pires da Silva I, Menzies AM, et al., Journal of Clinical Oncology 2020 (multicenter retrospective, provides the mutation‑specific ORR and PFS/OS mentioned above).
- Non‑V600 class II/III summaries: Menzer C et al., Eur J Cancer 2019 (clinical outcomes with MEK inhibitors); Dankner M et al., Clin Cancer Res 2018 (mutation‑specific sensitivity); Yao Z et al., Nature/Sci Transl Med 2017 (mechanistic basis).

Limitations and recency
- My knowledge is current through late 2024. Several 2024 conference updates and early‑phase trials further explore ERK and pan‑RAF strategies for class II/III BRAF; the directional conclusions above have been consistent across recent meetings. For the most up‑to‑date subgroup numbers (especially V600E vs V600K within each modern regimen), check the latest supplemental tables of:
  - COMBI‑d/v pooled long‑term update,
  - COLUMBUS 5–7‑year updates,
  - Pires da Silva/Menzies cohort publications and follow‑ups.

If you want, I can assemble a brief evidence table with direct quotes/figures and links from each source (for example, ORR/PFS/OS by mutation in each trial and cohort) once you indicate whether PubMed/DOI links or trial registry links are preferred.
