How relevant are these for collaboration? A quick guide
- Highest collaboration density: NeurIPS (Dec 2–7, San Diego) due to size, breadth, and workshops. At least 3 specific workshops are listed above (Dec 6–7 and Dec 6), each with focused communities—excellent for starting joint projects or reproducibility studies.
- Strong regional for ML: ACML (Dec 9–12, Taipei) — good mix of academia/industry in Asia, smaller scale supports deeper conversations.
- NLP focus: NLPIR (Dec 12–14, Fukuoka) and NLPTA (Dec 20–21, Sydney) — practical-size venues for initiating collaborations in NLP/IR; NLPIR’s proceedings (Springer indicated in CFP) help visibility.
- Multimedia/vision: ICSM (Dec 19–21, Paris), DPPR (Dec 20–21, Sydney). If your project has multimedia/vision components, these provide a focused audience.
- Generative AI/industry links: AIGC (Dec 27–28, Hangzhou) often attracts practitioners—useful for applied collaborations or dataset partnerships.
- Virtual options (3 events on Dec 18–19) let you schedule short talks or posters and line up 1:1s without travel; efficient if your December calendar is tight.

Notes on deadlines and practicality (as of 2025-11-27 UTC)
- Many December events have passed submission deadlines (e.g., NeurIPS main DL was mid-May; ACML late June; several others early Nov/Dec). However, registration to attend is typically still possible—useful for networking, recruiting collaborators, or proposing informal BoF meetups.
- For events with upcoming deadlines in early December (e.g., NLPTA/DPPR/DSA DL Dec 6; AIGC DL Dec 10; IEEE-MLCIPR DL Dec 12; ICCIT DL Dec 8), you may still submit short papers, demos, or workshop abstracts if aligned with your project timeline.

