2025-12-01 11:50:56,899 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Below is a concise, source-backed primer on Axios interceptors, followed by vetted options and numbers for related retry/caching tooling and strong alternatives with hook systems. All claims include concrete defaults, behaviors, or signatures you can verify at the linked sources.

What Axios “interceptors” are and how they work
- Two kinds, both global or per-instance:
  - Request: axios.interceptors.request.use(onFulfilled, onRejected, { synchronous?: boolean, runWhen?: (config) => boolean })
  - Response: axios.interceptors.response.use(onFulfilled, onRejected)
  - .use(...) returns an interceptor ID; remove with .eject(id).
  - Instance scope: const client = axios.create(...); client.interceptors.request/response.use(...)
  - Options:
    - synchronous (boolean; default false)
    - runWhen(config) (predicate; default always true)
  Sources: Axios docs (Interceptors, API, and examples): https://axios-http.com/docs/interceptors; broader docs: https://axios-http.com

Concrete patterns and numbers from Axios docs
- Retry-after-refresh (response interceptor):
  - Check error.response?.status === 401, call refreshToken(), then return instance(error.config) to retry the original request.
  - Source example: https://axios-http.com/docs/interceptors (see response error handling pattern)
- Interceptor removal:
  - const id = axios.interceptors.request.use(...); axios.interceptors.request.eject(id)
  - Source: https://axios-http.com/docs/interceptors
- Cancellation (modern and deprecated):
  - Preferred: AbortController (Axios v0.22+). Pass { signal: controller.signal } and call controller.abort().
  - Deprecated: CancelToken; avoid in new code. axios.isCancel(error) exists for distinguishing cancelation.
  - Node helpers: AbortSignal.timeout(ms) available on Node v17.3+.
  - Source: https://axios-http.com/docs/cancellation
- Node adapter details with specific defaults and knobs:
  - Redirects: maxRedirects default is 21; set 0 to disable. beforeRedirect hook is available.
  - Proxies: proxy: { protocol, host, port, auth } and honors http_proxy, https_proxy, no_proxy env vars. proxy: false disables proxy use.
  - Agents: httpAgent/httpsAgent for keep-alive pooling. Note: keepAlive is enabled by default on Node v19+ global agents.
  - UNIX sockets: socketPath is mutually exclusive with proxy.
  - Insecure HTTP parser toggle: insecureHTTPParser: boolean (use with caution).
  - Per-request rate-limits: maxRate: [uploadBytesPerSec, downloadBytesPerSec] (Node adapter).
  - Sources: https://axios-http.com/docs/req_config and transport sections; summary mirrored at https://axios-http.com/docs/intro and related pages
- Browser progress:
  - onDownloadProgress(progressEvent) is supported.
  - Source: https://axios-http.com/docs/req_config#onDownloadProgress
- Error-handling structure and serialization:
  - error.response (non-2xx), error.request (no response), error.message; error.toJSON() includes message, name, config, code, status, data, headers.
  - validateStatus controls resolve/reject for statuses.
  - Source: https://axios-http.com/docs/handling_errors
- Method aliases (all instance- or global):
  - get, delete, head, options, post, put, patch, postForm, putForm, patchForm; plus axios.request(config).
  - Source: https://axios-http.com/docs/api_intro

Ecosystem add-ons that use interceptors (with concrete defaults)
- axios-retry (GitHub: softonic/axios-retry)
  - Default retries: 3 (i.e., up to 3 attempts after the initial failure).
  - Default retryCondition: retries network errors or 5xx responses for idempotent methods (GET, HEAD, OPTIONS, PUT, DELETE).
  - retryDelay: default 0 ms; built-ins include exponentialDelay and linearDelay().
  - Per-request overrides via 'axios-retry' in request config.
  - Hooks: onRetry(retryCount, error, requestConfig) and onMaxRetryTimesExceeded(error, retryCount).
  - Source: https://github.com/softonic/axios-retry (README: options and defaults)
- axios-cache-interceptor (GitHub: arthurfiorette/axios-cache-interceptor)
  - Activation: setupCache(axiosOrInstance, options?)
  - Default cached methods: GET and HEAD. Responses have res.cached boolean.
  - Interceptor order is defined: request interceptors added before setupCache run before the cache; ones added after run after the cache (response is inverse due to FIFO/FILO).
  - Controls: per-request { cache: { ttl, methods, hydrate, update } }, id overrides, cachePredicate (headers/status/body matching), staleIfError predicate, manual storage ops.
  - Multiple storage backends (memory default; also persistent options).
  - Source: https://github.com/arthurfiorette/axios-cache-interceptor (Docs in README)
- Apisauce (Infinite Red)
  - Built on Axios but provides transforms/monitors:
    - addRequestTransform / addAsyncRequestTransform (mutate request globally)
    - addResponseTransform / addAsyncResponseTransform (mutate response; only response.data is mutable)
    - Monitors: addMonitor(fn) (read-only, run before promise resolves)
  - Response shape contains ok (boolean) and problem code strings (CLIENT_ERROR, SERVER_ERROR, TIMEOUT_ERROR, etc.), duration (ms), status, headers.
  - Source: https://github.com/infinitered/apisauce (README: transforms, monitors, response model)
- Xior (fetch-based Axios-like with interceptors)
  - Interceptors: interceptors.request.use/eject/clear and interceptors.response.use/eject/clear.
  - Built-in plugins: retry/throttle/dedupe; token refresh plugin (xior-auth-refresh) with queueing/one-time refresh.
  - Source: https://github.com/suhaotian/xior (README: interceptors and auth-refresh)

Alternatives to Axios with comparable hook/interceptor functionality
- Got (Node.js HTTP client)
  - Default retry config:
    - limit: 2 attempts
    - methods: GET, PUT, HEAD, DELETE, OPTIONS, TRACE
    - statusCodes: 408, 413, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504, 521, 522, 524
    - errorCodes: ETIMEDOUT, ECONNRESET, EADDRINUSE, ECONNREFUSED, EPIPE, ENOTFOUND, ENETUNREACH, EAI_AGAIN
    - backoff formula: ((2 ** (attemptCount − 1)) * 1000) + noise (default noise: 100 ms)
  - Hooks: init, beforeRequest, beforeRedirect, afterResponse (can refresh tokens and retry), beforeError, beforeRetry (with error and retryCount).
  - Per-request retry override via retry: { limit, statusCodes, errorCodes, calculateDelay, ... }.
  - Source: https://github.com/sindresorhus/got (README sections: Retry, Hooks)
- Ky (Fetch-based, browsers/Node/Bun/Deno)
  - Hooks oriented to retries and response handling:
    - beforeRequest(request, options, { retryCount })
    - beforeRetry({ request, options, error, retryCount }): can return ky.stop to halt further retries or a Response to short-circuit.
    - afterResponse(request, options, response, state): can call ky.retry({ delay, request, code }) to force a retry (e.g., token refresh or rate-limit handling).
    - beforeError(error, state) to customize thrown errors.
  - Source: https://github.com/sindresorhus/ky#hooks
- Superagent (HTTP client)
  - Plugins via .use(plugin) per-request or per-agent, plus lifecycle events ('request', 'redirect', 'response', 'progress', 'error').
  - Redirects: follows up to 5 redirects by default; configurable via .redirects(n) or .redirects(0) to disable.
  - Retries: .retry(n) or .retry(n, (err, res) => boolean) for custom logic; err.retries indicates attempts.
  - Source: https://github.com/ladjs/superagent (README: Plugins, Redirects, Retries)
- node-fetch and Undici (Fetch implementations; no “interceptors” in Fetch spec, but Undici offers interceptor modules)
  - node-fetch: supports AbortController, per-request agent, redirect modes, compression, and stream control; no interceptor API.
    - Source: https://github.com/node-fetch/node-fetch (README: usage, abort, redirects)
  - Undici: Node’s high-performance HTTP stack; has interceptors.cache for HTTP caching:
    - Compose an Agent with interceptors.cache({ store, methods: ['GET', 'HEAD'] }) and set as global dispatcher; respects Cache-Control, Expires, ETag/Last-Modified validation.
    - Example configuration uses MemoryCacheStore; example numbers shown in docs: maxSize 100 MB, maxCount 1000, maxEntrySize 5 MB (configuration examples, not global defaults).
    - Node 18+ ships fetch built on Undici; can set global dispatcher or origin.
    - Source: https://undici.nodejs.org (docs: Interceptors and Cache Interceptor)

If you’re focused specifically on Axios interceptors, here are practical, source-backed starting points
- Add/remove interceptors and scope to instances:
  - axios.interceptors.request.use(fn, errFn, { synchronous: false, runWhen: () => true })
  - axios.interceptors.request.eject(id); same for response.
  - Source: https://axios-http.com/docs/interceptors
- Typical response-interceptor retry flow (token refresh):
  - On 401, await refreshToken(), then return instance(error.config) to retry the original request once the header/state is updated.
  - Source: https://axios-http.com/docs/interceptors (error handling examples)
- Node behavior with exact defaults:
  - maxRedirects: 21 by default; set to 0 to disable.
  - Proxy: honors http_proxy/https_proxy/no_proxy; set proxy: false to ignore env vars.
  - Keep-alive: enabled by default on Node v19+ global agents; otherwise configure httpAgent/httpsAgent.
  - Source: https://axios-http.com/docs/req_config and adapter sections

Recommendations, depending on your needs (all link to verifiable docs)
- Need built-in retries with controllable backoff: axios-retry (retries: default 3; exponentialDelay provided). Source: https://github.com/softonic/axios-retry
- Need robust HTTP caching with fine control and interceptor order awareness: axios-cache-interceptor (default caches GET/HEAD; res.cached flag; TTL, invalidate/update APIs). Source: https://github.com/arthurfiorette/axios-cache-interceptor
- Prefer a fetch-based client with strong retry hooks and forced retries: Ky (beforeRetry/afterResponse/ky.retry). Source: https://github.com/sindresorhus/ky#hooks
- Need Node-first client with explicit and well-documented retry defaults and hooks: Got (retry.limit default 2; precise statusCodes/errorCodes/backoff formula). Source: https://github.com/sindresorhus/got#retry
- Want Axios semantics but with higher-level transforms and monitors: Apisauce (request/response transforms, response.ok/problem classification). Source: https://github.com/infinitered/apisauce
- Want a minimal Axios-like with interceptors plus batteries (retry/throttle/dedupe, token refresh plugin): Xior. Source: https://github.com/suhaotian/xior
- Rely on Fetch but want caching/interceptor-like behavior: Undici interceptors.cache (standards-based cache semantics). Source: https://undici.nodejs.org

Minimal, verifiable example (Axios interceptors + axios-retry)
- Interceptors (from Axios docs):
  - const id = axios.interceptors.request.use(cfg => cfg, err => Promise.reject(err));
  - axios.interceptors.request.eject(id)
- axios-retry defaults (from README):
  - import axiosRetry from 'axios-retry'
  - axiosRetry(axios, { retries: 3, retryDelay: axiosRetry.exponentialDelay })

Cited sources index
- Axios official docs (Interceptors, Cancellation, Error handling, Request config): https://axios-http.com/docs/interceptors; https://axios-http.com/docs/cancellation; https://axios-http.com/docs/handling_errors; https://axios-http.com/docs/req_config
- axios-retry (defaults and options): https://github.com/softonic/axios-retry
- axios-cache-interceptor (usage, defaults, order): https://github.com/arthurfiorette/axios-cache-interceptor
- Apisauce (transforms, monitors, response model): https://github.com/infinitered/apisauce
- Xior (interceptors and auth-refresh): https://github.com/suhaotian/xior
- Got (retry defaults, hooks): https://github.com/sindresorhus/got#retry and https://github.com/sindresorhus/got#hooks
- Ky (hooks and retries): https://github.com/sindresorhus/ky#hooks
- Superagent (plugins, redirects default 5, retries): https://github.com/ladjs/superagent#plugins; https://github.com/ladjs/superagent#redirects; https://github.com/ladjs/superagent#retrying-requests
- node-fetch (AbortController, redirects, agents): https://github.com/node-fetch/node-fetch
- Undici (interceptors.cache, agents/dispatcher, global fetch): https://undici.nodejs.org

If you want, tell me your exact environment (browser/Node version, framework, and whether you need retries/caching/auth refresh), and I’ll map these patterns to a minimal, production-ready setup with the relevant defaults preconfigured.
