Interceptors
- Request interceptors: Modify outgoing config (e.g., attach Authorization from localStorage). You can chain multiple request interceptors.
  - http.interceptors.request.use(fn)
  - Remove one: interceptors.request.eject(handler)
  - Clear all: interceptors.request.clear()
- Response interceptors: Process data and handle errors globally (e.g., log TypeError, clear token on 401).
  - http.interceptors.response.use(onFulfilled, onRejected)
  - Remove one: interceptors.response.eject(handler)
  - Clear all: interceptors.response.clear()

Advanced interceptor use cases
- Encryption/decryption: In request interceptor, set header and transform req.data to encrypted blob; in response interceptor, decrypt res.data.blob and JSON.parse it.
- Customizing retry key: In a request interceptor, inject a derived key into config.params (e.g., params.___k from headers['x-custom-field']) to influence error-retry plugin uniqueness.
- Custom response parsing: Set responseType: 'custom', then parse based on Content-Type in a response interceptor (support JSON/text, etc.); skip empty bodies.

Auth token refresh
- Community plugin (xior-auth-refresh): Install xior-auth-refresh, define refreshAuthLogic (e.g., POST to refresh, save token, update failedRequest.response.config.headers.Authorization), and createAuthRefreshInterceptor(xior, refreshAuthLogic). Failed 401s are retried after refresh.
- Built-in plugins: Use token-refresh with error-retry.
  - Attach token in a request interceptor.
  - shouldRefresh(response): return true for 401/403 when a token exists.
  - errorRetry({ enableRetry: (config, error) => shouldRefresh(error.response) })
  - setupTokenRefresh(instance, { shouldRefresh, async refreshToken(error) { try POST /token/new; set new token; on failure delete token and reject } })

Mocking with MockPlugin
- Setup: const mock = new MockPlugin(instance)
- Basic replies:
  - mock.onGet('/users').reply(status, data, headers)
  - mock.onPost('/users', body?, { params? }).reply(...)
- Conditional matching:
  - By params: mock.onGet('/users', { params: { searchText: 'John' }})
  - By body (POST): mock.onPost('/users', { searchText: 'John' })
  - By regex: mock.onGet(/\/users\/\d+/).reply(config => [200, {}])
- Chaining: mock.onGet('/users').reply(200, users).onGet('/posts').reply(200, posts)
- Reply once: .replyOnce(...) auto-removes handler after first match; subsequent matches fall through to next handler or 404.
- Pass-through:
  - Specific matches: .passThrough() forwards matched requests to network
  - Fallback for unmatched: mock.onAny().passThrough()
  - Default passthrough mode: new MockPlugin(instance, { onNoMatch: 'passthrough' })
- Reject all GET with 500: mock.onGet().reply(500)
- Handlers access and reset:
  - mock.handlers provides registered handlers (e.g., mock.handlers['get'])
  - mock.reset() clears handlers; mock.resetHandlers() also clears all registered mocks

Plugins lifecycle
- Register: const handler = instance.plugins.use(plugin())
- Eject one: instance.plugins.eject(handler)
- Clear all: instance.plugins.clear()

Stability/performance plugins
- error-retry: retries failed GETs to improve resilience
- error-cache: returns cached data when retries still fail (prevent crashes)
- dedupe: prevents concurrent identical GETs; onDedupe(config) callback
- throttle: throttles identical requests within a threshold (default 1000 ms); onThrottle(config) callback
  - Enable for POST by enableThrottle: true or by marking request as isGet: true
  - Use custom threshold per request (e.g., { throttle: 2000 })
  - Use useCacheFirst: true to return cached data immediately and refresh in background
  - Example options (defaults):
    - threshold: number (1000)
    - enableThrottle: boolean | (config) => boolean (default: GET or config.isGet)
    - throttleCache: ICacheLike (default tiny-lru with 100 items)
    - throttleItems: numbe
Tool `Context7:get-library-docs` with Parameter {'context7CompatibleLibraryID': '/infinitered/apisauce', 'topic': 'request/response transforms monitors', 'tokens': 4000} on Context7 succeeded. Result (summarized from 2881 tokens): SUMMARIZED CONTENT:

Overview of key Apisauce features and workflows

- Creating an API instance
  - create({ baseURL, headers, timeout }) to configure base URL, default headers, and timeout (e.g., 30000 ms).
  - Can wrap a pre-configured axios instance: create({ axiosInstance }).

- Quick start
  - Make requests (e.g., GET to GitHub API) and use the response data.
  - Per-request headers are supported via the third argument to HTTP methods.

- Calling API methods
  - Supported: get, head, delete, post, patch, put, link, unlink, and any (generic).
  - any accepts an axios-style config object: { method, url, params, data, headers, ... }.

- Response handling model
  - Response object:
    - ok: boolean (true for HTTP 200–299; false otherwise).
    - problem: string (one of the problem codes below).
    - data: parsed response body.
    - status: HTTP status code.
    - headers: response headers.
    - config: axios config used for the request.
    - duration: milliseconds elapsed for the request.
    - originalError: the original axios error (for debugging when needed).

- Problem codes (exposed as constants on the API instance)
  - NONE: null (HTTP 200–299) — no problems.
  - CLIENT_ERROR: 'CLIENT_ERROR' (HTTP 400–499).
  - SERVER_ERROR: 'SERVER_ERROR' (HTTP 500–599).
  - TIMEOUT_ERROR: 'TIMEOUT_ERROR' — server didn’t respond in time.
  - CONNECTION_ERROR: 'CONNECTION_ERROR' — server not available/bad DNS.
  - NETWORK_ERROR: 'NETWORK_ERROR' — network not available.
  - CANCEL_ERROR: 'CANCEL_ERROR' — request canceled (requires providing cancelToken in config).

- Adding request monitors
  - api.addMonitor(fn): monitor functions run before the returned promise resolves, allowing inspection without modification.
  - Exceptions thrown in monitors are caught internally and won’t crash your app.

- Adding request transforms (global request modification)
  - Synchronous: api.addRequestTransform(request => { ... }).
  - Asynchronous: api.addAsyncRequestTransform(async request => { ... }) — executed sequentially.
  - You can modify method, url, headers, params, and data (e.g., add/remove params, change headers, alter URL paths, conditionally mutate body).

- Adding response transforms (global response modification)
  - Synchronous: api.addResponseTransform(response => { ... }).
  - Asynchronous: api.addAsyncResponseTransform(async response => { ... }).
  - You receive a response object (data, headers, duration, problem, etc.); only response.data is mutable.

- Changing request headers
  - Persist across the API instance:
    - setHeader(name, value)
    - setHeaders({ ... }).
  - Can still override via per-request config.

- Changing base URL
  - setBaseURL(newUrl) to update dynamically.
  - getBaseURL() to read current base URL.

- Canceling requests
  - Use CancelToken:
    - const source = CancelToken.source()
    - Pass { cancelToken: source.token } in the request config.
    - source.cancel() to abort.

- Using async/await
  - Works seamlessly: const response = await api.get(...); check response.ok and other fields.

- Mocking with axios-mock-adapter
  - Create Apisauce API and pass its internal axios instance to MockAdapter:
    - const mock = new MockAdapter(api.axiosInstance)
  - Mock responses (e.g., mock.onGet(...).reply(200, ...)) and test behavior.

- Installing Apisauce from a GitHub branch
  - For testing local/remote changes in React/React Native projects:
    - yarn install <git remote url>

- Project tooling (for contributors)
  - Bypass git hooks: git commit --no-verify (use sparingly; fix lint/compile later).
  - Run tests: yarn test
  - Lint and compile: yarn lint && yarn compile
  - TSLint rules reference: https://palantir.github.io/tslint/rules/

