2025-12-01 12:15:15,718 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Below is a data‑driven look at how commonly discussed fruits stack up for protein, carbohydrates, and sugars against widely cited nutrition guidelines. I highlight jackfruit in particular because it’s often promoted as a “higher‑protein fruit,” then show how it compares to banana, guava, mango, orange, apple, and avocado.

What the major guidelines say (carbs and sugars)
- Carbohydrates (adults, general population)
  - Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA): 130 g/day minimum for brain glucose needs (Institute of Medicine, 2005).
  - Acceptable Macronutrient Distribution Range (AMDR): 45–65% of total calories from carbs. On a 2,000‑kcal diet, that’s ~225–325 g/day (Institute of Medicine, 2005).
- Added sugars
  - Dietary Guidelines for Americans (DGA) 2020–2025: keep added sugars <10% of calories (i.e., <50 g/day at 2,000 kcal) (USDA/HHS, 2020).
  - WHO (2015): free sugars <10% of energy; a conditional target of <5% is suggested for additional benefit (i.e., <50 g/day, ideally <25 g/day at 2,000 kcal). Note: “Free sugars” exclude sugars in intact whole fruit (WHO, 2015).
  - American Heart Association (2009): upper limit women ≤100 kcal/day (~25 g) and men ≤150 kcal/day (~36 g) of added sugar (AHA/Circulation, 2009).
Important: these sugar limits apply to “added” (DGA, AHA) or “free” sugars (WHO). Naturally occurring sugars in whole, intact fruit are not “added sugar,” and WHO explicitly excludes sugars in intact fruit from “free sugar.”

Fruit-by-fruit macronutrients (per 100 g edible portion)
Source for numbers: FruityVice API (values align closely with USDA FoodData Central; minor variation by variety/ripeness). Calories rounded.

- Jackfruit: 95 kcal; carbohydrates 23.2 g; sugars 19.1 g; protein 1.72 g
- Banana: 96 kcal; carbohydrates 22.0 g; sugars 17.2 g; protein 1.0 g
- Guava: 68 kcal; carbohydrates 14.0 g; sugars 9.0 g; protein 2.6 g
- Mango: 60 kcal; carbohydrates 15.0 g; sugars 13.7 g; protein 0.82 g
- Orange: 43 kcal; carbohydrates 8.3 g; sugars 8.2 g; protein 1.0 g
- Apple: 52 kcal; carbohydrates 11.4 g; sugars 10.3 g; protein 0.3 g
- Avocado: 160 kcal; carbohydrates 8.53 g; sugars 0.66 g; protein 2.0 g

How jackfruit “stacks up”
- Protein: At 1.72 g/100 g, jackfruit is higher than many fruits but still modest. For context, a typical adult’s protein need is ~0.8 g/kg/day (Institute of Medicine, 2005); a 70‑kg person needs ~56 g/day. You’d need ~3.3 kg of jackfruit to reach 56 g protein—so jackfruit is not a meaningful protein source compared with legumes, dairy, eggs, tofu/tempeh, or meats.
- Carbohydrates: 23.2 g carbs per 100 g. Relative to the 130 g/day RDA, 100 g jackfruit provides ~18% of the minimum; relative to the AMDR (225–325 g/day at 2,000 kcal), it provides ~7–10% of a day. A practical portion of ~150 g (about 1 cup of arils; estimate) would give ~35 g carbs.
- Sugars: 19.1 g sugars per 100 g (≈29 g per ~150 g). These are naturally occurring sugars in intact fruit, so they do not count toward added sugar limits (DGA, AHA) and are not “free sugars” per WHO. If you personally track total sugar for glycemic reasons, note that jackfruit is on the higher side among fruits for natural sugars.

Comparisons if you’re prioritizing protein and/or limiting sugars
- Most protein per 100 g among these: guava (2.6 g) ≈ avocado (2.0 g) > jackfruit (1.72 g) > orange/banana (~1 g) > mango (0.82 g) > apple (0.3 g).
- Lowest natural sugars per 100 g: avocado (0.66 g) ≪ orange (8.2 g) < guava (9.0 g) < apple (10.3 g) < mango (13.7 g) < banana (17.2 g) < jackfruit (19.1 g).
- If you want higher protein with comparatively less sugar per carb, guava outperforms jackfruit among fruits. If your priority is minimal sugars, avocado and citrus are better choices. Banana and jackfruit are more carb/sugar-dense per 100 g.

Putting it into your daily targets
- Carbohydrate target examples:
  - If you aim near the RDA minimum (130 g/day), a ~150 g portion of jackfruit (~35 g carbs) uses ~27% of your daily carb budget.
  - If you aim for ~225–325 g/day (2,000 kcal AMDR), that same portion is ~11–16% of your daily carbs.
- Added sugars: Whole fruit sugars are not “added sugars,” so jackfruit (and other intact fruit) does not reduce your allowed added sugar budget under DGA/AHA, nor count as “free sugars” under WHO.
- Protein: Even generous fruit portions contribute little toward daily protein needs. For meeting protein goals, pair fruit with higher‑protein foods (e.g., yogurt, cottage cheese, eggs, tofu, tempeh, legumes, protein‑rich grains).

Bottom line
- Jackfruit can fit a healthy diet but is not a strong protein source. It is relatively high in natural sugars and carbohydrates compared with many fruits, so portion size matters if you are carb‑restricting or managing glycemia. For more protein per gram and/or lower sugars from fruit, guava and avocado respectively are stronger choices.
- If you share your specific numeric carb and sugar targets (e.g., grams/day or % of calories), I can translate any fruit portion you like into precise contributions toward those goals.

Sources (primary/official)
- Institute of Medicine (now National Academies). Dietary Reference Intakes for Energy, Carbohydrate, Fiber, Fat, Fatty Acids, Cholesterol, Protein, and Amino Acids (2005). Carbohydrate RDA 130 g/day; AMDR 45–65% energy; protein 0.8 g/kg/day. https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK56068/
- U.S. Departments of Agriculture and Health & Human Services. Dietary Guidelines for Americans, 2020–2025. Added sugars <10% of calories. https://www.dietaryguidelines.gov/sites/default/files/2020-12/Dietary_Guidelines_for_Americans_2020-2025.pdf
- World Health Organization. Guideline: Sugars intake for adults and children (2015). Free sugars <10% energy; conditional <5%; excludes sugars in intact fruit. https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241549028
- American Heart Association Scientific Statement. Johnson RK et al. Dietary Sugars Intake and Cardiovascular Health. Circulation. 2009;120:1011–1020. Women ≤100 kcal/day (~25 g) and men ≤150 kcal/day (~36 g) added sugar. https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIRCULATIONAHA.109.192627

Fruit nutrient data used
- FruityVice API (per 100 g values listed above; aligns closely with USDA FoodData Central for raw fruits). https://www.fruityvice.com/
- USDA FoodData Central (reference database for cross‑checking fruit nutrient values): https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/
